# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Swapping two variables".
id: swap-temp
title: Swapping two variables with a temporary variable
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Swapping two variables"
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, consequence

task:
  Compute by hand the weakest precondition of the following program, which
  swaps the values of $x$ and $y$ using a temporary variable $t$.
  Deduce that the program is correct with respect to its contract.

pre: x = A && y = B
post: x = B && y = A

program:
  {P}
  t := x;      // i1
  {R2}
  x := y;      // i2
  {R1}
  y := t       // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Read the specification
rule: SEQ
text:
  The precondition says that $x$ starts with some value $A$ and that $y$
  starts with some value $B$. The names $A$ and $B$ are logical variables.
  The program never assigns them, so they keep their values during the
  whole execution. They let the postcondition talk about the initial values
  of $x$ and $y$.

  The program is a sequence of three assignments. The rule SEQ needs one
  intermediate assertion between every two statements. We call them
  $R1$ and $R2$ in the annotated program.

  We reason backwards. We start from the postcondition $Q$, we compute
  $R1$ from the last statement, then $R2$, and then the weakest
  precondition of the whole program. Finally we compare it with $P$.

step: The last statement, y := t
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
text:
  The assignment rule says that the precondition of `y := t` is the
  postcondition with every occurrence of $y$ replaced by $t$.
calc:
  wp(y := t, Q)
  = Q[y <- t]
  = (x = B && y = A)[y <- t]
  = x = B && t = A        // only the y of y = A is replaced
set: R1 = x = B && t = A
instance: ASSIGN
  ---
  {x = B && t = A} y := t {x = B && y = A}
text:
  The variable $x$ does not change, so the conjunct $x = B$ stays as it is.
  The conjunct $y = A$ becomes $t = A$: after the assignment $y$ holds the
  value of $t$, so $y = A$ holds after it exactly when $t = A$ holds before it.

step: The second statement, x := y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  We now use $R1$ as the postcondition of `x := y`.
calc:
  wp(x := y, R1)
  = (x = B && t = A)[x <- y]
  = y = B && t = A
set: R2 = y = B && t = A
instance: ASSIGN
  ---
  {y = B && t = A} x := y {x = B && t = A}

step: The first statement, t := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(t := x, R2)
  = (y = B && t = A)[t <- x]
  = y = B && x = A
instance: ASSIGN
  ---
  {y = B && x = A} t := x {y = B && t = A}
text:
  This is the weakest precondition of the whole program:
  $wp(t := x; x := y; y := t, Q) = (y = B && x = A)$.

step: Compare the precondition with the weakest precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
vc: x = A && y = B ==> y = B && x = A
why:
  Both sides contain the same two conjuncts, in a different order.
  A conjunction does not depend on the order of its conjuncts, so the
  implication holds.
instance: CONSEQUENCE
  x = A && y = B ==> y = B && x = A
  {y = B && x = A} t := x; x := y; y := t {x = B && y = A}
  x = B && y = A ==> x = B && y = A
  ---
  {x = A && y = B} t := x; x := y; y := t {x = B && y = A}
text:
  The postcondition did not change, since we started the computation from
  $Q$ itself. So the third premise of the consequence rule, $Q ⇒ Q$, is
  trivially true.

step: Put the proof together
rule: SEQ
instance: SEQ
  {x = A && y = B} t := x {y = B && t = A}
  {y = B && t = A} x := y; y := t {x = B && y = A}
  ---
  {x = A && y = B} t := x; x := y; y := t {x = B && y = A}
text:
  The first premise follows from the triple of the first statement and the
  implication of the previous step. The second premise follows from the
  triples of the second and third statements, again with the rule SEQ.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  We computed the intermediate assertions backwards, one assignment at a
  time, and the weakest precondition was the precondition itself, with the
  conjuncts in a different order.
